I added a new table, {{provider_estimate}}. Do not confuse it with {{preparation_estimates}}(which is not used, and will be dropped soon).

The new source of data should be something like this. For now, it should be ok to use the old format for orders with no entries in {{provider_estimate}}.

* *Initial preparation time*
** {{select provider_display_duration_seconds from provider_estimate where master_basket_id = ? and provider_order_state = 'waiting_confirmation'}}
* *Corrected preparation time*
** {{select provider_display_duration_seconds from provider_estimate where master_basket_id = ? and provider_order_state = 'accepted'}}
* *Preparation time used for dispatching:* The preparation time that we sent as an input to the Total ETA model on provider acceptance
** Still retrieved from the eta table, since the Total ETA (for the Eater) and the Provider ETA are computed in 2 different places.
** {{select preparation_time_predicted_seconds from eta where master_basket_id = ? and type = ‘ACCEPTED' and is_event_flow_entry is true}}
* *Target courier arrival time at the merchant*: The output from the optimal courier arrival time model
** From the new Optimal courier arrival time table
* *Provider Busy:* A flag that shows whether the provider had activated busy mode at the time of the order
** {{select is_provider_busy from provider_estimate where master_basket_id = ? and provider_order_state = 'waiting_confirmation'}}
* *ML corrections enabled:* The flag that shows whether ML was disabled or not (the “Disable ETA correction“ flag on provider profile)
** {{select disable_estimation_correction from provider_estimate where master_basket_id = ? and provider_order_state = 'waiting_confirmation'}}

Hey [~accountid:712020:0b1ccfcb-3809-460a-88ab-c96985ba329a] - thanks - I want to align with you on 1 point re/ South Africa:

I remember from previous discussions in the team call and your update in [Slack|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C07QL43ERFF/p1745481584097819] you mentioned that hard opt-in is only recognised in ZA, soft opt-in is not recognised.

However, below in your message you've clarified that by virtue of our ongoing relationship with active Drivers, we can send them marketing comm’s, without consent, subject to an opt-out being included - this effectively is the definition of soft opt-in.

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*For clarity, soft opt-in* is a professional term (often not defined under privacy laws, including POPIA) which refers to an exception where marketing may be sent *without prior hard opt-in consent* if certain conditions - similar to those set out in *Section 69(3) of POPIA* - are met, such as:

* The recipient’s contact details were obtained in the context of existing customer relationship/sale;
* The marketing is for similar products or services offered by the same entity; and
* The recipient was given a clear opportunity to opt out, both at the time of data collection and in every subsequent marketing message.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
I’ve researched the law and it seems that:

# The recent clarifications of POPIA [you've flagged|https://taxify.slack.com/archives/C07QL43ERFF/p1745481584097819] only apply to the rules that mandate hard opt-in consent.
# Soft opt-in consent remains a possibility under POPIA - per section 69(3).

*Please confirm your alignment that soft opt-in is in fact permissible in ZA.*
{panel}

Lets align on terminology to avoid confusion in the future - you can check the definitions in this page for future reference - [https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LEG/pages/5195661552/Direct+Marketing+Communication+Privacy+Requirements|https://taxify.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/LEG/pages/5195661552/Direct+Marketing+Communication+Privacy+Requirements|smart-link] 🙌

